+
+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions for concurrent visit edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers edit the same plot visit from tablets that may be offline for up to 48 hours, so two workers routinely start from the same visit state and submit days apart. The record store must decide how those concurrent edits become the current record. Arrival order is not evidence of authority, and no original submission may be discarded or silently overwritten (HLD tenet: *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*). Whatever is chosen sets the submission metadata tablet-sync sends, the shape of coordinator review work, and which record states review-export may include in the partner CSV.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scientific-meaning safety** — *major*. The mechanism must not combine edits in ways nobody notices. A visit's fields are scientifically related (a species identification and its cover estimate, an observer list and the notes that attribute work); rules keyed on field names cannot see those relationships. Turns on the HLD tenets *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** — *major*. Whichever mechanism ships must be maintained by the team that runs the product as it evolves (visit schema changes, new fields, observer-list edits). Turns on the HLD tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Review workload before the Friday export** — *moderate*. Two coordinators resolve review work weekly; more competing-edit volume means more comparisons before export.
+- **Coordinator review comprehensibility** — *moderate*. The review interface must let a coordinator see what differs and choose confidently without specialized tooling. Turns on the two-coordinator tenet above.
+- **Tablet storage and submission shape** — *moderate*. Tablets keep drafts across 48-hour disconnections; either full payloads or base snapshots plus structured changes must fit comfortably on shared devices.
+
+A latent criterion considered and cut: *speed of first submission* — both options let a worker submit offline without re-fetching, so they score equally and the element does not discriminate.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every visit has a numbered revision. Each submission carries the base revision ID the worker started from and a complete visit payload — the whole record as the worker last saw it, edited. If the base matches the current revision, the payload becomes the next revision. If another revision has since become current, the submission is still accepted and retained as a competing complete revision; the visit is marked as having unresolved review work. A coordinator compares the competing revisions and selects one as the new current revision. Nothing is merged field-by-field, and no original submission is ever dropped.
+
+**Demonstration.** Visit sits at revision 3. Tablet A and Tablet B both fetched revision 3, both go offline, both edit. A submits first: revision 4 (current). B submits with base 3: retained as a competing revision; the visit flags unresolved work. A coordinator compares A's and B's payloads, selects one, and revision 5 carries the winner. Both original submissions remain in history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Scientific-meaning safety — **strong**. Competing revisions arrive intact; a coordinator always sees whole submissions and never inherits an automated combination of related fields.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden — **strong**. Acceptance is a base-revision comparison plus retention; there are no merge rules to maintain as the visit schema evolves.
+- Review workload before the Friday export — **weak**. Edits to unrelated fields still produce competing revisions and review work; the queue is larger than a merge scheme would produce.
+- Coordinator review comprehensibility — **strong**. The review surface is a comparison of two complete visits — the same object the field worker saw.
+- Tablet storage and submission shape — **strong**. The tablet keeps a base revision ID (a few bytes) and sends the payload it already holds.
+
+**Summary.** Buys safety and simplicity with a deliberately larger review queue.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each tablet keeps a base snapshot of the visit it fetched and submits structured changes (per-field deltas) rather than a whole payload. On arrival the server three-way merges: changes to disjoint fields combine automatically; changes that overlap go to review. Combining scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries requires maintained merge rules, and the review/export side needs field-level provenance so a coordinator can see which submission supplied each value.
+
+**Demonstration.** Same scenario: A corrects an observer name; B adds a missing species. Disjoint fields — the server combines both automatically and no review work is created. Had both revised the same species' cover estimate, the overlap would go to review with field-level provenance showing each submission's contribution.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Scientific-meaning safety — **weak**. Disjoint-field combination can cross scientific relationships the rules do not model: an identification revised in one submission and its cover estimate revised in another are disjoint by field name but related scientifically, and would merge without anyone noticing.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden — **weak**. Merge rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries must be built and maintained as the schema changes; provenance tracking adds a second maintained surface.
+- Review workload before the Friday export — **strong**. Unrelated edits never reach review; only true overlaps do.
+- Coordinator review comprehensibility — **partial**. Overlap review needs field-level provenance views; the coordinator must trust that non-overlapping combinations were correct.
+- Tablet storage and submission shape — **partial**. Base snapshots plus change structures are heavier to keep consistent across long offline periods and app updates than a revision ID and a payload.
+
+**Summary.** Buys a smaller review queue with merge rules whose correctness on related fields the system cannot verify.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.** It wins the two major criteria outright: no mechanism ever combines scientifically related edits without a coordinator seeing the whole submissions, and there are no merge rules for two coordinators to maintain. It also wins review comprehensibility and tablet shape. It loses review workload — a larger queue of competing revisions, including conflicts over unrelated fields — and that loss is accepted deliberately: a visible queue a coordinator can work through beats an invisible combination the system cannot audit.
+
+Committed, the choice obligates downstream:
+
+- **tablet-sync** must track a base revision ID per draft, send it with each whole-payload submission, and surface pending competing revisions to the field worker.
+- **review-export** must provide a revision comparison workflow for competing revisions and exclude visits with unresolved review work from the export set until a coordinator resolves them.
+
+It forecloses silent field-level combination for this release; a visit never becomes export-ready by server-side merging.
+
+The decision turns on *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and is taken against the efficiency preference a merge scheme would serve.
+
+**Why the other approach would still be considered.** Field-aware three-way merge remains the credible alternative if experience contradicts the weights here: if the review queue demonstrably outruns two coordinators before the Friday export, or if the field-level provenance review-export builds for comparison work turns out to make merge rules cheap to maintain, the trade should be re-run. Any revisit must still pass the preservation gate — no original submission may be discarded — and should expect to re-examine the scientific-meaning criterion, which is where this option loses.
