+## Revision Model
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+Concurrency follows whole-visit revisions (see [decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md)):
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+- Each accepted visit payload becomes a numbered revision. The first accepted submission creates revision 1.
+- Every submission names the base revision ID the worker started from (`0` for the submission that creates revision 1).
+- When the base revision ID equals the visit's current revision, the submitted payload becomes the next revision.
+- When the base revision ID is behind the current revision, the submission is still accepted and retained in full as a competing revision. The visit is marked as having unresolved review work. The pre-conflict revision remains current until a coordinator resolves.
+- Competing revisions may stack: any number of submissions may accumulate against a visit with unresolved work, and all of them are retained.
+- A base revision ID the server has never issued, or one ahead of the current revision, is rejected as invalid; it cannot name a starting state the worker actually fetched.
+
+Original submissions are never rewritten, merged, or discarded. The current revision is a pointer to one retained payload, not a synthesis of others.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+### Submission envelope
+
+Each submission carries:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| visitId | Client-generated UUID identifying the visit |
+| baseRevisionId | Revision the worker fetched before editing (`0` = creating revision 1) |
+| payload | The complete visit record: plot UUID, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes |
+| idempotencyKey | Client-generated key making retry safe across connection failures |
+
+The server authenticates the submitting worker and records the author ID and server receipt time alongside the stored payload. It validates field types and that the plot UUID exists in the catalog before accepting. The payload is preserved separately from any later resolution outcome.
+
+Idempotency: a retry with an accepted key and an identical payload returns the original acceptance result without creating another stored submission; reuse of an accepted key with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged.
+
+### Retrieval
+
+Current-state retrieval returns:
+
+- the current revision (revision ID and payload),
+- whether unresolved review work exists for the visit,
+- when unresolved: the competing revisions — each with its submission ID, author ID, server receipt time, base revision ID, and payload.
+
+The current revision does not change when a competing submission arrives, so retrieval always has a stable value to return alongside the unresolved-work indicator. Submission-history retrieval returns every stored submission for the visit in receipt order, including those already resolved, so the path from any submitted observation to the current record stays inspectable.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves unresolved review work by selecting one revision — the current revision or any competing submission — as the winner. The resolution:
+
+- records the coordinator's identity,
+- records the contributing submission IDs (every competing submission the resolution settles),
+- records the selected submission as the source of the winning payload,
+- issues the next revision ID carrying that payload, and
+- clears the visit's unresolved-work flag.
+
+Resolution selects whole submitted payloads; it does not compose fields from multiple submissions. After resolution, later submissions are compared against the new current revision as usual. If a stale-base submission arrives while a resolution is in flight, it is retained as a competing revision against whatever revision is current when it is accepted, and the visit returns to unresolved work. A second resolution attempted against a visit that another coordinator has already resolved is rejected; the requester re-fetches and re-evaluates.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: submissions name a base revision ID; competing complete revisions await coordinator resolution ([decision doc](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md)) | Field-aware three-way merge: base snapshots plus structured changes, disjoint fields combined automatically | Merge rules keyed on field names can combine scientifically related edits without anyone noticing; the accepted cost is a larger review queue |
+| Stale-base submissions | Accept and retain as competing revisions; never reject a worker's edit for being based on an old revision | Reject with a stale-revision error, requiring the worker to re-fetch before editing | Tablets are offline up to 48 hours; rejection discards the worker's editing window and conflicts with keeping field workers collecting observations without connectivity |
+| Resolution composition | Coordinator selects one existing revision as the winner | Coordinator authors a fresh payload combining preferred values from competing revisions | Authoring a fresh payload is field-level combination by hand at resolution time; selection keeps every exported value traceable to a submission |
+
+## Downstream Obligations
+
+These obligations originate in this node's contract. Each is owned by the sibling node that must accommodate it when that node is designed:
+
+- **tablet-sync** retains a base revision ID per draft, sends it with each whole-payload submission, and surfaces pending competing revisions to the field worker so a stale-base outcome is explainable on the device.
+- **review-export** provides a revision comparison workflow over competing submissions and excludes visits with unresolved review work from the export set until a coordinator resolves them.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Neither sibling may invent its own concurrency semantics; both consume the envelope, retrieval shape, and resolution operation defined here.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+### Resolved
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy for concurrent visit edits: whole-visit revisions, recorded with full tradeoffs in [decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md).
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+1. Retention and compaction policy for resolved and losing submissions — first release retains all submissions indefinitely per the preservation requirement; pruning is not needed at 6,000 visits per season yet.
 
